  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Trevor McDonald lasted three innings. After that, the bullpen held Miami scoreless for 4.1 innings. But Miami's four-run fourth. The only inning that mattered. Defined the game before the Giants ever led.",
    "what_this_means": "They left fourteen runners on base and converted two of them into runs. Casey Schmitt drove in both. Ten hits and ten strikeouts. The bats made noise but couldn't sustain it. The offense got runners in scoring position fourteen times and found nothing after the third inning. That's the problem: they're inconsistent within the same game. Good at-bats exist alongside empty stretches, and by the time they find their timing, the window has closed. Schmitt's home run was real. The other thirteen runners were just history. They needed runs from multiple sources and got them from one.",
    "what_to_watch": "Ryan Gusto comes in with a 7.24 ERA. Logan Webb's last four starts carry a 0.66 ERA. Tonight at 10:40 AM PT, the matchup says the Giants should hit it early.",
